Besides that, we select Q&A data for training as it closely matches the format utilized during user-oriented inference. We sampled a subset from the Alpaca dataset to serve as harmless data. For the harmful data, we followed the process outlined by Shaikh et al. (2023), which utilized GPT-3.5 to generate a set of harmful questions and answers. Finally, we add the corresponding tag after each answer. In this stage, the prompt is composed of an instruction i and a question q, i.e., $\mathrm{x} = (\mathrm{i},\mathrm{q})$ . The target sequence is denoted as $\mathrm{y} = (\mathrm{a},\mathrm{t})$ where $\mathrm{a} = (y_{1},\dots ,y_{l})$ is the answer and $\mathrm{t} = (y_{l + 1},\dots ,y_{m})$ denotes the tag tokens. We tune the LLM with the same loss function shown in Equation (1).

Compared to the current safety training, which relies on the model rejecting harmful questions i.e., $p_{\theta}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{i},\mathbf{q})$ , SELF-GUARD allows the LLM using additional information from the response, i.e., $p_{\theta}(t|\mathbf{i},\mathbf{q},\mathbf{a})$ . This decreases the difficulty of detecting harmfulness.

